 ## Overview
-Cell Reconstruction via Octree Hashing is a method for locating leaf nodes and retrieving adjacent "forward" neighbor voxels in a sparse octree volume representation, enabling isosurface reconstruction from scalar data stored with only one value per leaf voxel [S1, S2, S3]. It solves the problem of needing 8 corner values for a cell intersection primitive when only 1 scalar value is stored per leaf node by retrieving the 7 forward neighbors at intersection time [S2, S3]. Unlike [Cell Retrieval via Neighbor-Finding](1822-cell-retrieval-via-neighbor-finding.md), which relies on neighbor-finding techniques that skew heavily toward O(1) performance when neighbors share a parent node, this method uses a bitwise arithmetic hash scheme for point location, allowing point location from the root in O(log N) time [S1, S2, S3].
+Cell Reconstruction via Octree Hashing locates leaf nodes and retrieves adjacent forward neighbor voxels in a sparse octree volume, enabling isosurface reconstruction from scalar data stored with only one value per leaf voxel [S1, S2, S3]. It solves the requirement for 8 corner values in a cell intersection primitive by retrieving the 7 forward neighbors at intersection time using a bitwise arithmetic hash scheme for point location [S2, S3]. Unlike [Cell Retrieval via Neighbor-Finding](1822-cell-retrieval-via-neighbor-finding.md), which relies on spatial locality to skew toward O(1) performance, this method guarantees O(log N) point location from the root without explicit parent pointers [S1, S2, S3].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** 3D integer coordinates $(x, y, z)$ representing a voxel center or corner, and the root of the sparse octree.
-*   **Output:** The leaf node containing the coordinate, or the set of 7 forward neighbor leaf nodes required to define the 2x2x2 cell for intersection testing.
